      mechanism: { eyebrow:"Technical anatomy", title:"Two paths. One interrupted contract.", intro:"This defensive model visualizes state transitions—not exploit code. It explains why request parsing, cleanup, worker reuse and application authentication sit on opposite sides of the failure boundary.", cve1:"CVE-2026-109618 · OOM corruption", cve1s:"Request-owned state becomes reachable before initialization is complete. A controlled OOM leaves invalid data that shutdown may read or act upon.", cve2:"CVE-2026-109619 · Stale global state", cve2s:"Request-owned state is published in worker-wide globals. The request heap is freed, but the pointer survives and is consumed by a later request.", contract:"The interrupted contract", contractText:"State published → fallible allocation or fatal path → early exit → cleanup or next request trusts stale state → memory disclosure / RCE", app:"Application code", appNote:"May never run", parser:"HTTP parser", parserNote:"Multipart / upload", heap:"Request heap", heapNote:"Partially initialized", bailout:"OOM / fatal", bailoutNote:"longjmp / abrupt exit", cleanup:"Shutdown cleanup", cleanupNote:"Reads invalid state", worker:"Same worker", workerNote:"Later request", auth:"Authentication", authNote:"Not reached", boundary:"REQUEST / WORKER BOUNDARY", legend:"The application may authenticate correctly—and still be irrelevant. The flaw executes in the runtime before user code." },
      sapi: { eyebrow:"Runtime divergence", title:"The SAPI changes the blast radius", intro:"All web-serving SAPIs are in scope. Upstream advisories publish fixes for maintained 8.2–8.5 branches; researchers separately assess older paths back to PHP 5.4. The simplest pre-application path is strongest in Apache mod_php, where request shutdown still runs after startup fails.", mod:"Apache mod_php", mods:"Highest risk", modd:"Startup failure still invokes request shutdown; the invalid request state is consumed in the same request.", fpm:"PHP-FPM", fpms:"Conditional path", fpmd:"A startup failure exits the child. Other application-specific and multi-request state-lifecycle paths remain relevant.", others:"php-cgi · FrankenPHP · LiteSpeed", othersd:"Exposure depends on request startup, shutdown and worker-reuse behavior. Verify each deployed SAPI rather than inferring safety from a different topology." },
      exposure: { eyebrow:"Global surface", title:"The long tail is the real attack surface", intro:"Internet scans count different things. The robust conclusion is not a single headline number: millions of PHP services are visible, and a large minority run branches that are already unsupported.", modat:"Modat scan", modatv:"4.38M PHP services", modatd:"1.89M unique hosts · 79% on unsupported branches, as reported from a scan snapshot around 20 October.", vul:"VulnCheck scan", vulv:"2.61M hosts", vuld:"More than 1.1M on EOL branches; only 403K on actively maintained 8.2–8.5 branches, according to the reported scan.", censys:"Censys scan", censysv:"56.6M web properties", censysd:"3.08M hosts detected running PHP; another snapshot measuring a different unit of exposure.", verdict:"Operational verdict", verdictd:"Patch supported branches now. Isolate or retire EOL runtimes. Inventory embedded and appliance-bundled PHP. Verify the runtime that serves traffic—not only the CLI binary.", eol:"Lifecycle pressure", eold:"PHP 8.2 reaches EOL on 31 December 2026. A security patch is a bridge, not an architecture decision." },
      potassium: { eyebrow:"Threat activity", title:"Potassium: an attempted delivery chain", intro:"The Mirai-derived family was linked to PHP2Shell scanning and loader delivery attempts. Researchers documented the chain; widespread successful PHP2Shell takeover was not publicly confirmed in the cited early reporting.", chain:"Attack chain", loader:"ELF loader server", c2:"C2 channel", flood:"DDoS targets", warn:"Evidence discipline", warnd:"Indicators below are defensive research IOCs. Do not probe them from production networks. Treat them as blocklist candidates only within an approved change process." },
      response: { eyebrow:"Incident-response playbook", title:"Preserve first. Patch completely. Hunt backward.", intro:"A four-stage response sequence synthesized from official PHP guidance and national CERT advisories. Where sources conflict, the official vendor advisory controls.", s1:"01 · Preserve evidence", s1d:"Isolate or place behind a restrictive WAF. Capture VM/container snapshots and live RAM before restarting PHP workers, because restart destroys heap and memory-disclosure evidence.", s2:"02 · Update and restart", s2d:"Move supported branches to PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 or 8.2.35. Rebuild images and restart every PHP worker. EOL systems need verified vendor backports or network isolation.", s3:"03 · Apply temporary controls", s3d:"Disable session upload progress and PHP 8.5 fatal backtraces where appropriate. Enforce decoded request limits at the edge. Never substitute RLIMIT_AS or RLIMIT_DATA for PHP limits.", s4:"04 · Hunt and rotate", s4d:"Search from 17 October. Correlate memory-limit errors, unusual multipart shapes, large PHPSESSID values and web-server children spawning shells. Rotate secrets after the exposure window.", matrix:"Patch matrix", directive:"Directive / control", value:"Value", why:"Purpose", caveat:"Caveat", c1:"session.upload_progress.enabled", v1:"0 / Off", w1:"Blocks session upload-progress paths", x1:"Temporary measure; restart workers", c2:"fatal_error_backtraces", v2:"0", w2:"Removes confirmed PHP 8.5 producer", x2:"Does not cover every global-state path", c3:"memory_limit", v3:"-1 (admin-enforced)", w3:"Avoids predictable PHP OOM threshold", x3:"Not complete; pair with cgroup/container isolation", c4:"post_max_size + edge limits", v4:"Conservative", w4:"Limits request / multipart inflation", x4:"Only mitigates OOM strategy; patch is mandatory", c5:"RLIMIT_AS / RLIMIT_DATA", v5:"Do not use", w5:"Avoids triggering the same allocation failure", x5:"Not a PHP security control" },
      findings: { eyebrow:"Strategic findings", title:"Four lessons beyond the patch", f1:"AI compresses exploit economics", f1d:"Defensive AI research shortened the path from a difficult memory-safety flaw to a reproducible exploit. The same capability lowers the cost for attackers.", f2:"Embargo is not a trust boundary", f2d:"The disclosure network included vendors, distributors, researchers and defenders. The operational assumption must be that exploitation can begin before the advisory.", f3:"Absence of logs is not absence of breach", f3d:"A request can fail before application logging starts. Patch the runtime, then still assess memory-resident credentials and rotate them.", f4:"Open-source maintenance is systemic risk", f4d:"Critical infrastructure depends on a small maintenance community. Funding, review capacity and decision mechanics are part of the attack surface." },
